## What it is

`Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection` (MEDI) is the container behind every generic-host app. It is deliberately small: constructor injection only, three lifetimes, open generics, `IEnumerable<T>` for multiple registrations, and — since .NET 8 — keyed services. Senior interviews rarely ask "what is DI"; they ask what happens when lifetimes interact badly and who disposes what.

## How it works underneath

- **Lifetimes.** `Transient` creates a new instance per resolution. `Scoped` creates one instance per `IServiceScope` (ASP.NET Core opens one scope per request and exposes it as `HttpContext.RequestServices`). `Singleton` creates one instance for the root provider, lazily on first resolution (or eagerly if you registered an instance).
- **Call-site resolution.** On first request for a service the provider builds a *call site* (a tree of constructor calls), then compiles it (expression trees or IL emit; reflection-based under Native AOT). Later resolutions reuse it, so resolution is cheap but not free.
- **Multiple registrations.** `GetService<IFoo>()` returns the **last** registration; `IEnumerable<IFoo>` returns all of them in registration order. `TryAdd*` skips if the service type is already registered; `TryAddEnumerable` skips only if the same service/implementation pair exists.
- **Registration identity.** Each registration owns its own instance. `AddSingleton<IReader, FileStore>()` plus `AddSingleton<IWriter, FileStore>()` yields **two** `FileStore` singletons. To share one, register the concrete type and forward: `AddSingleton<IReader>(sp => sp.GetRequiredService<FileStore>())`.
- **Keyed services (.NET 8+).** `AddKeyedSingleton<ICache, RedisCache>("redis")`, resolved via `[FromKeyedServices("redis")]` or `IKeyedServiceProvider.GetRequiredKeyedService<ICache>("redis")`. Keyed registrations are invisible to un-keyed resolution; `KeyedService.AnyKey` registers a fallback for any key.
- **Disposal.** The container disposes every `IDisposable`/`IAsyncDisposable` **it created**, when the owning scope (or the root, at shutdown) is disposed, in **reverse order of creation**. It does not dispose instances you passed in (`AddSingleton(myInstance)`). Disposing a scope synchronously when a scoped service implements only `IAsyncDisposable` throws `InvalidOperationException`; use `CreateAsyncScope()` + `await using`.

## Captive dependency

A longer-lived service holding a shorter-lived one: a singleton that takes a scoped `DbContext` in its constructor captures the first instance forever, shares it across concurrent requests, and leaks its change tracker. The host enables `ValidateScopes` and `ValidateOnBuild` **only in the Development environment**; there you get "Cannot consume scoped service ... from singleton ..." at `Build()`. In Production the same code starts fine and fails later under concurrency. A transient injected into a singleton is also captive, just less visibly.

The fix is never "make it singleton too". In singletons and `BackgroundService`s, inject `IServiceScopeFactory` and create a scope per unit of work:

```csharp
public sealed class OutboxPublisher(IServiceScopeFactory scopes, ILogger<OutboxPublisher> log)
    : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        using var timer = new PeriodicTimer(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
        while (await timer.WaitForNextTickAsync(ct))
        {
            await using var scope = scopes.CreateAsyncScope();   // one scope per batch
            var db = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<AppDbContext>();
            var bus = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IMessageBus>();
            await PublishPendingAsync(db, bus, ct);
        }                                                       // scope disposed: DbContext released
    }
}
```

## What an interviewer probes

- The three lifetimes and *where* a scope comes from outside HTTP (you create it).
- Captive dependencies, why Development catches them and Production doesn't.
- Transient `IDisposable` resolved from the **root** provider: the root tracks it until shutdown — a memory leak in a loop. Resolve from a scope or don't make it container-owned.
- Why the service-locator style (`IServiceProvider` injected everywhere) is a smell, and where it is legitimate (factories, hosted services, middleware with scoped deps via `InvokeAsync` parameters).
- Disposal order and `IAsyncDisposable` handling.

## Common misconceptions

- "Transient objects are garbage-collected, so disposal does not matter" — container-created disposables are tracked by their scope.
- "Scoped means per-thread" — it means per `IServiceScope`; `async` code hops threads freely within one scope.
- "One implementation type = one singleton" — one registration = one singleton.

