## What it is

Observability in .NET rests on three BCL primitives that OpenTelemetry (OTel) exports without wrapping them: **logs** via `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger`, **traces** via `System.Diagnostics.ActivitySource`/`Activity`, and **metrics** via `System.Diagnostics.Metrics.Meter` (`Counter<T>`, `Histogram<T>`, `UpDownCounter<T>`, observable gauges). The OTel SDK subscribes to these APIs and ships the data over OTLP to a collector or backend. Your code depends only on the BCL types.

## How it works underneath

**Structured logging.** `logger.LogInformation("Order {OrderId} placed by {CustomerId}", id, cust)` passes a *message template* plus arguments. Providers receive the template and the named values separately, so a backend can index `OrderId` and group all occurrences of one template. An interpolated string (`$"Order {id} placed"`) is formatted eagerly — even when the level is disabled — produces a distinct message per value, and loses the property names (analyzer CA2254 flags it). The extension methods also allocate a `params object[]` and box value types on every call. The `[LoggerMessage]` source generator removes that: it emits a strongly-typed method that checks `IsEnabled` first, uses a cached parsed template and avoids boxing.

**Scopes.** `using (logger.BeginScope(new Dictionary<string, object> { ["TenantId"] = t }))` attaches properties to every log written inside the `using`, including across `await` (scopes flow through `AsyncLocal`). Whether they are *emitted* is the provider's choice: console formatters and the OTel logging provider have `IncludeScopes`, off by default.

**Traces.** An `Activity` is a span. `ActivitySource.StartActivity(name)` returns **null** when no listener is sampling that source — the zero-cost path when tracing is off — so every use must be null-safe (`activity?.SetTag(...)`). `Activity.Current` flows through `ExecutionContext`, so child spans nest automatically across `await`. ASP.NET Core and `HttpClient` create activities and propagate W3C `traceparent` headers; the logging infrastructure stamps `TraceId`/`SpanId` on log records, which is what lets you jump from a log line to its trace.

**Metrics.** Instruments are created from a `Meter` (via `IMeterFactory` in DI since .NET 8). Each unique combination of tag values is a separate time series, so tags must have **bounded cardinality** (route template, status code, region) — never user id, order id or raw URL. The OTel SDK caps series per metric and folds the excess into an overflow series, silently degrading your data. Since .NET 8 ASP.NET Core and `HttpClient` emit built-in metrics (`http.server.request.duration`, etc.) following OTel semantic conventions.

```csharp
public sealed partial class PaymentService(ILogger<PaymentService> log, IMeterFactory meters)
{
    public static readonly ActivitySource Source = new("Shop.Payments");
    private readonly Histogram<double> _duration =
        meters.Create("Shop.Payments").CreateHistogram<double>("shop.payment.duration", unit: "s");

    public async Task ChargeAsync(Order order, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        using var activity = Source.StartActivity("Charge");   // null if nobody listens
        activity?.SetTag("payment.provider", order.Provider);
        var start = Stopwatch.GetTimestamp();
        await _gateway.ChargeAsync(order, ct);
        _duration.Record(Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(start).TotalSeconds,
            new KeyValuePair<string, object?>("payment.provider", order.Provider)); // bounded tag
        LogCharged(log, order.Id, order.Total);
    }

    [LoggerMessage(EventId = 1001, Level = LogLevel.Information, Message = "Charged order {OrderId} for {Amount}")]
    private static partial void LogCharged(ILogger logger, Guid orderId, decimal amount);
}

// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry()
    .WithTracing(t => t.AddSource("Shop.Payments").AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation().AddHttpClientInstrumentation())
    .WithMetrics(m => m.AddMeter("Shop.Payments").AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation())
    .UseOtlpExporter();
```

## What an interviewer probes

- Template vs interpolation, and the cost of logging on hot paths.
- Logs vs traces vs metrics: what question each answers (what happened / where time went across services / how much and how often).
- How a trace id crosses process boundaries (W3C Trace Context) and how logs get correlated with it.
- Sampling (head-based in the SDK vs tail-based in the collector) and its effect on what you can debug.
- Metric cardinality and why the source name passed to `AddSource`/`AddMeter` must match exactly.

## Common misconceptions

- "Interpolated strings are fine now that C# has interpolated string handlers" — the `ILogger` extension methods do not take one.
- "`StartActivity` always returns an activity" — not without a listener.
- "More tags on metrics = more insight" — high-cardinality tags belong on spans and logs.
- "Scopes are lost after `await`" — they flow; the provider may just not print them.

