## What it is

The **generic host** (`Host.CreateApplicationBuilder`, or `WebApplication.CreateBuilder` which builds on it) owns the application's DI container, configuration, logging and **lifetime**. Long-running work plugs in as `IHostedService`; `BackgroundService` is the abstract base class most code should use. The host is also what turns `SIGTERM`/Ctrl+C into an orderly shutdown.

## How it works underneath

**Startup.** `host.RunAsync()` builds nothing new — it calls `StartAsync` on every registered `IHostedService`, **sequentially in registration order**, awaiting each. In ASP.NET Core your hosted services start **before** Kestrel starts listening, so a slow `StartAsync` delays the whole app (and its readiness). .NET 8 added `HostOptions.ServicesStartConcurrently`/`ServicesStopConcurrently` (default `false`), `HostOptions.StartupTimeout` (default infinite), and `IHostedLifecycleService` with `StartingAsync`/`StartedAsync`/`StoppingAsync`/`StoppedAsync` hooks. After all services start, `IHostApplicationLifetime.ApplicationStarted` fires.

**BackgroundService.** Its `StartAsync` kicks off `ExecuteAsync(stoppingToken)`, stores the task and returns without awaiting it. Historically the synchronous part of `ExecuteAsync` (everything before the first real `await`) ran inline on the startup path, so a blocking loop with no `await` hung startup; .NET 10 changed `BackgroundService` to run all of `ExecuteAsync` as a separate task. `StopAsync` cancels `stoppingToken` and waits for `ExecuteAsync` to finish **or** for the host's shutdown token to fire, whichever comes first.

**Failures.** Since .NET 6, an unhandled exception escaping `ExecuteAsync` is logged and, under the default `BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior.StopHost`, **stops the host**. Before .NET 6 it was swallowed silently and the worker simply died. `Ignore` restores the old behaviour — rarely what you want; catch and handle per-iteration exceptions inside the loop instead.

**Graceful shutdown.** `SIGTERM` (Kubernetes, `docker stop`), `SIGINT` or `IHostApplicationLifetime.StopApplication()` trigger: `ApplicationStopping` fires → hosted services' `StopAsync` run in **reverse registration order** (Kestrel stops accepting connections and drains in-flight requests) → `ApplicationStopped` → the container is disposed. The whole stop phase is bounded by `HostOptions.ShutdownTimeout` — **30 seconds by default since .NET 6** (5 s before). Work still running when it elapses is abandoned when the process exits.

In containers, two things must line up: the app must actually receive the signal (exec-form `ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "App.dll"]`, not a shell wrapper that swallows `SIGTERM`), and `ShutdownTimeout` must be shorter than Kubernetes `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` (default 30 s), after which the kubelet sends `SIGKILL`. Readiness should flip to unhealthy as soon as stopping begins so the load balancer stops routing new traffic.

```csharp
builder.Services.Configure<HostOptions>(o => o.ShutdownTimeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(25));
builder.Services.AddHostedService<QueueWorker>();

public sealed class QueueWorker(IQueueClient queue, IServiceScopeFactory scopes, ILogger<QueueWorker> log)
    : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        await foreach (var msg in queue.ReadAllAsync(stoppingToken))
        {
            try
            {
                await using var scope = scopes.CreateAsyncScope();
                await scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IHandler>().HandleAsync(msg, stoppingToken);
                await queue.AckAsync(msg, CancellationToken.None);   // finish the ack even while stopping
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested) { break; }
            catch (Exception ex) { log.LogError(ex, "Message {MessageId} failed", msg.Id); }  // keep the worker alive
        }
    }
}
```

## What an interviewer probes

- `IHostedService` vs `BackgroundService`, and why `StartAsync` must not run long work.
- What happens to an exception in `ExecuteAsync` (and the .NET 6 behaviour change).
- The shutdown sequence and timeouts, end to end from `SIGTERM` to process exit.
- Using scoped services from a hosted service (scope per unit of work).
- At-least-once processing: what happens to an in-flight message when the process is killed.

## Common misconceptions

- "Hosted services start after the server" — in ASP.NET Core they start before it.
- "The host waits for my `ExecuteAsync` to finish" — only up to `ShutdownTimeout`.
- "An exception in a `BackgroundService` crashes the process" — it stops the host gracefully (by default) after logging; it does not tear the process down mid-flight.

## What it is

A senior engineer is expected to have a **method**, not a bag of tricks: measure at the system level, narrow down with a profiler, prove a fix with a micro-benchmark, then verify in the real system. .NET gives you a cross-platform toolbox for each step, all built on **EventPipe** (in-process event streaming, no kernel driver needed, works in Linux containers).

## The tools, from coarse to fine

- **`dotnet-counters`** — live, low-overhead metrics from a running process: CPU, working set, GC heap size, gen0/1/2 counts, `% time in GC`, allocation rate, thread-pool thread count and **queue length**, lock contention, exception rate; plus ASP.NET Core and Kestrel meters. `dotnet-counters monitor -p <pid> --counters System.Runtime,Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting`. It answers "what kind of problem is this?" (CPU-bound, GC-bound, starvation, exceptions) — not "which method?".
- **`dotnet-trace`** — collects an EventPipe trace (`.nettrace`) with CPU sampling (periodic managed stack samples) and/or runtime events (GC, allocations, exceptions, contention). `--format speedscope` gives a flame graph; PerfView and Visual Studio open `.nettrace` directly. The `gc-verbose` profile includes `AllocationTick` events — one sampled event roughly every 100 KB allocated, with type and stack — which is the cheap way to find *who allocates* in production.
- **`dotnet-gcdump`** — heap snapshot (types, counts, retention) for "what is on the heap"; **`dotnet-dump`** — full process dump for offline analysis (`clrstack`, `dumpheap`, `syncblk`), ideal for hangs and deadlocks. In Kubernetes run them from a sidecar or `kubectl debug` ephemeral container sharing the process namespace, or bake them into a diagnostics image.
- **BenchmarkDotNet** — micro-benchmarks done correctly. It builds a Release copy of your benchmarks and runs them in **a separate process**, performs pilot and warm-up iterations so you measure steady-state code (after tiered compilation and Dynamic PGO have promoted it to tier 1), runs many iterations and reports mean, error, standard deviation and, with `[MemoryDiagnoser]`, **allocated bytes per operation** and GC counts per 1,000 operations. `[Baseline]` gives ratios; `[Params]` sweeps inputs; returning the result prevents the JIT from eliminating dead code.

```csharp
[MemoryDiagnoser]
public class ParseBench
{
    private const string Input = "id=42;qty=7;price=1999";

    [Benchmark(Baseline = true)]
    public int Split() => Input.Split(';').Sum(p => int.Parse(p.Split('=')[1]));

    [Benchmark]
    public int Span()
    {
        int sum = 0;
        foreach (var range in Input.AsSpan().Split(';'))           // .NET 9+ MemoryExtensions.Split
        {
            var part = Input.AsSpan()[range];
            sum += int.Parse(part[(part.IndexOf('=') + 1)..]);
        }
        return sum;                                                 // returned: no dead-code elimination
    }
}
// dotnet run -c Release -- --filter *ParseBench*
```

## Common hot paths interviewers expect you to recognise

- Allocation in loops: LINQ over small collections, closures capturing locals, `string` concatenation, `params` arrays, boxing (struct to interface, non-generic collections, `string.Format` with value types).
- Exceptions as control flow (throwing is microseconds, not nanoseconds).
- Rebuilding expensive objects per call: `new Regex(..., RegexOptions.Compiled)`, `HttpClient` per request (socket exhaustion), reflection-based lookups; cache them or use source generators (`[GeneratedRegex]`, System.Text.Json source generation).
- Sync-over-async and thread-pool starvation — visible in counters as a growing queue length with low CPU.
- Large or mid-lived allocations causing gen2/LOH collections (see the memory topic).

## What an interviewer probes

- Your sequence: symptom → counters → trace/dump → hypothesis → benchmark → fix → verify with the same counters under load.
- Why a `Stopwatch` loop lies (JIT tiers, no warm-up, Debug builds, dead-code elimination, noise).
- How to profile in production safely (sampling overhead, short collections, `--duration`).
- How to read `[MemoryDiagnoser]` output.

## Common misconceptions

- "Average latency is the metric" — percentiles (p95/p99) and allocation rate tell the story.
- "Micro-benchmark wins translate linearly to the service" — only if that path is actually hot in the profile.
- "Profilers must be attached at startup" — EventPipe tools attach to a running process.
